## 5. IAM Permissions

The IAM role `lambda_execution_role` was created with a Lambda trust policy.

The following managed policies were attached:

- `AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole` — basic CloudWatch logging permissions.
- `NautilusS3CopyDynamoDBPolicy` — permissions to read objects from the public bucket, write objects to the private bucket, and write records to DynamoDB.

The custom policy was successfully created and attached to the execution role.

## 6. Lambda Configuration

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Function name | `nautilus-copyfunction` |
| Runtime | Python 3.12 |
| Handler | `lambda-function.lambda_handler` |
| Memory | 128 MB |
| Timeout | 10 seconds |
| Region | `us-east-1` |

The Lambda function uses Boto3 to interact with S3 and DynamoDB. It reads the source bucket and object key from the S3 event, copies the object to the private bucket, and writes a log entry with a unique `LogID`.

## 7. DynamoDB Log Structure

The DynamoDB table `nautilus-S3CopyLogs` uses `LogID` as its string partition key.

Each successful transfer log includes:

| Attribute | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `LogID` | Unique identifier for the log record |
| `SourceBucket` | Name of the source S3 bucket |
| `DestinationBucket` | Name of the destination S3 bucket |
| `ObjectKey` | Name or key of the copied object |
| `Timestamp` | Time the operation was logged |
| `Status` | Transfer outcome |

The function also attempts to record error details if a transfer fails.

## 8. Test Procedure

The test file was uploaded with:

```bash
aws s3 cp /root/sample.zip \
  s3://nautilus-public-938836777/sample.zip \
  --region us-east-1
```

The private bucket was checked using:

```bash
aws s3api head-object \
  --bucket nautilus-private-938836777 \
  --key sample.zip \
  --region us-east-1
```

The DynamoDB records were inspected using:

```bash
aws dynamodb scan \
  --table-name nautilus-S3CopyLogs \
  --region us-east-1
```

